[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fb01ad-fc9d-5c1d-96c5-0e78cb67f17e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3d27d1e-a375-5722-8f78-27b79ebf66c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343180" :question/problem "![](images/49/4946691a4258fb310ec7052f1edd5a1b9117ca7daeea152e4a1b01b11a624e2e.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe water level is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =50\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =25\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=50\\,\\text{cm}$ and $y=25\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(50)(25)z=1250z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1250z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}250 & =1250\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =0.2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is increasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260cd36f-3cd1-5e6e-a08e-7d3ca9370c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500542} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba08eda-a7a0-539e-92cd-414b03d79077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500722} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1e521-d9dc-5058-a940-b2513a2a3010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500832} {:answer/id #uuid "b17792ba-6da9-5712-933a-ecb6ba3164b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500837} {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e4a4bf-3e09-5621-be81-4a8b258251af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc2b2629-e323-5ab5-9be3-1b12132dec56" :question/math-academy-id "q-140379" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501496} {:answer/id #uuid "17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501512} {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} {:answer/id #uuid "711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501674} {:answer/id #uuid "dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} :answer-field/id #uuid "60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337311" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{C}\\right) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501645} {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501727} {:answer/id #uuid "cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501874} {:answer/id #uuid "e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336519" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501493} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501570} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501573} {:answer/id #uuid "79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501693} {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336746" :question/problem "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)+3{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a73b112-23d9-5ce7-b7f6-9cc9b1ea6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501541} {:answer/id #uuid "5173cabb-209f-59b0-bb30-f3195ff21e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501614} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cacfb-384f-5813-911b-4115a5b7ba6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} {:answer/id #uuid "8d016a81-6b30-515f-b97d-3efba5f2f640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501737} {:answer/id #uuid "f91702ba-eae7-53c8-b6a7-9bd48502b51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cacfb-384f-5813-911b-4115a5b7ba6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72de5c-8edd-59cf-9c2b-dc6c69c9659d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22282018-2ca4-523a-8e4c-90a2c94b5770" :question/math-academy-id "q-68493" :question/problem "${\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}={2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9-7}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501466} {:answer/id #uuid "24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501533} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501640} {:answer/id #uuid "850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501721} {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337573" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05436460-8fd0-5eaf-8d02-d2f20514c78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501478} {:answer/id #uuid "134d188d-b9c3-552f-b921-1183370fb0c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501501} {:answer/id #uuid "21a0e148-8730-5869-a663-8249c3f4a7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501523} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501799} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaec04f-5284-5518-89e1-55d6d0d563f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b9e3db-75fc-500b-be46-121b7aace8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501799} :answer-field/id #uuid "14daa8e3-e1bb-5707-81d3-5bbf9b88123d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185" :question/math-academy-id "q-357243" :question/problem "The temperature inside a freezer decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 4+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{5\\times 3}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{15}{20}=-\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501600} {:answer/id #uuid "531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501619} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501850} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} :answer-field/id #uuid "f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3933c79b-01f9-56d3-9429-e1ebc3d9a1ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337317" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C),$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\right)}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0064f3e5-7e46-560e-af4f-5f1bc6505542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.10\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501463} {:answer/id #uuid "457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.91\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501592} {:answer/id #uuid "6005b2ab-465f-5cd8-bb5d-d54c1eeed27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.35\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501642} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ea94bf-a047-5c90-b7d0-f7b804cd2312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.48\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501829} {:answer/id #uuid "d94e5053-03cf-5ba6-ada7-c621dbe03ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.72\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "457c4add-95f2-5630-82a0-e8ce4e4d81a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.91\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501592} :answer-field/id #uuid "752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502017}] :question/id #uuid "4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336685" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{35}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2268a62f-af3b-5554-9392-93550ebd3f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501524} {:answer/id #uuid "34f958da-773f-515f-abd1-d2b6edb53bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501563} {:answer/id #uuid "8075dcdc-4e30-5eab-9850-ab4d6e913258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501711} {:answer/id #uuid "a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501784} {:answer/id #uuid "ab74afde-81f9-5e89-9f65-02cca1c6532c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46483a5-c5e1-536a-a0d5-b280e918e22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501784} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae216ed6-c5e7-5d88-9e19-dcdfe886b809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" :question/math-academy-id "q-357241" :question/problem "The temperature of a chemical solution decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{15\\times 4}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{60}{20}=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501521} {:answer/id #uuid "41e819ff-cd32-5d60-ba16-54665c890b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.84\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501588} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba3054a-6459-569b-81c3-907904cb6174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.78\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501735} {:answer/id #uuid "91dfca2e-8991-5e9e-966d-e682738680c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.12\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501745} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa06dd-98f7-529e-8d06-b10b01e3a805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.05\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fd464ab-03dd-5c29-a547-e17631549a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501521} :answer-field/id #uuid "396eb760-1ba4-5c61-a4fd-b36963318cca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501992}] :question/id #uuid "9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336843" :question/problem "A $300\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{150}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501465} {:answer/id #uuid "26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501536} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501638} {:answer/id #uuid "c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501861} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952} :answer-field/id #uuid "507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336503" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501476} {:answer/id #uuid "0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501484} {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} {:answer/id #uuid "a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501795} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337315" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\right)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8297df33-3945-557a-8b12-47cba0ef0ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501715} {:answer/id #uuid "cf62465d-ee5a-56f9-9205-cdbc67e9ed15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501877} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b02612-0eeb-5065-9a7a-a11e62b8557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501924} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c706d3-0dd0-5ebe-87cc-56a68f59d32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501938} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c9430f-5d3d-51ad-9d23-0d3aa66de7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501948} :answer-field/id #uuid "2536844b-ad4a-5ed6-bce8-3971a2720b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" :question/math-academy-id "q-357085" :question/problem "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" :question/worked-solution "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=-\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 4}=-\\frac{8}{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035b5582-aa17-529b-b101-df7971bd9920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501471} {:answer/id #uuid "0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501485} {:answer/id #uuid "2881adf2-dc78-54da-838f-2275d55fed05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501539} {:answer/id #uuid "57cf6cec-0ed9-5d2e-af3f-5ae7420f7f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{76}{21}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501630} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bde92-27d8-57f7-b7da-25ad1a8a9ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{20}{7}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0792a188-0269-58cf-97bd-516b8fe89cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501485} :answer-field/id #uuid "7180b989-67d5-5e43-9459-6d581302e067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192" :question/math-academy-id "q-357194" :question/problem "A hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second. What is the total change in altitude after $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "The hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second.\n\nSince the altitude decreases, the change each second is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds, so the total change in altitude is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{9\\times 7}{7\\times 3}=-\\frac{9}{3}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in altitude is $-3\\,\\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98.74\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501692} {:answer/id #uuid "af336e1d-577f-5f06-a6fb-d40be71dd596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.90\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501812} {:answer/id #uuid "db9870bb-ca97-5434-9e2f-f0f1821c5287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.12\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501907} {:answer/id #uuid "dc627a42-82c5-5373-81cf-850a6f244c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.83\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501909} {:answer/id #uuid "f405b4b6-a9a5-5b98-9592-9a6a03980d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.65\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78a9c22a-d2cf-5556-83bc-973ce5081e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98.74\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501692} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501998}] :question/id #uuid "ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336840" :question/problem "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+110}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{24}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501618} {:answer/id #uuid "80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501713} {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} {:answer/id #uuid "e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501929} {:answer/id #uuid "f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337313" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a455916-32b4-5125-a03d-acadaf8f7915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502359} {:answer/id #uuid "74dbaa7e-fca7-5073-a877-6b16ff3e4d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502367} {:answer/id #uuid "8a06c96f-4a15-5bf2-a6d0-edbdb248951e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502372} {:answer/id #uuid "90fb3517-b11c-5317-ac7d-17e978e3d179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502376} {:answer/id #uuid "c45caa27-da31-57ac-8666-1a0bb5c8d193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45caa27-da31-57ac-8666-1a0bb5c8d193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502404} :answer-field/id #uuid "139bd750-f127-56f4-9c8c-dad3ecb6aa91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "15405bfd-218a-5ebb-ac44-be3eba615aca" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d62b7f63213e0a36a4cc186c396de6986f8c9e594fa045791d9c37fad7550f60.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/ae/ae31ae08afde8173954eef7609108f51e2a5113e7ef37a815511d29e653f33bf.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44542f74-71e7-507c-ba2a-4a28f636ac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502352} {:answer/id #uuid "57d39e45-b7dc-5b05-accd-6ded543fb154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502357} {:answer/id #uuid "c55ad23b-68a1-557a-aa55-3f07c42db8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502405} {:answer/id #uuid "e16574a4-34e8-54cf-a77e-3ad1ef0fe437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502420} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b27dcd-fc04-5048-9e40-7aa9d5043980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16574a4-34e8-54cf-a77e-3ad1ef0fe437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502420} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d9330f-8252-5b46-9c3b-ad958ccbb399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25059bd6-40b8-5009-96b3-70ae023f7607" :question/problem "![](images/35/35ce1ab5c1689dca152c58e955e4a43c0be0e2bc6a796bf20e84f2c99786f8ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b906579f40ce2a1c59129d80540b339c1bb00fcd9a5f5ce179b01efeb702ab5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=3.5$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aeab7be-8c7d-5a3a-be9f-120546db36cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502337} {:answer/id #uuid "78dffdfd-3b80-57ec-a318-d95ddef548d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502369} {:answ