&= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7829f8-6eb6-557a-9168-c1c357d9482c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7829f8-6eb6-557a-9168-c1c357d9482c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503878} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dad12b4-53d9-5ebb-9ae6-bd1949a26915" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f6eb79d-ca08-5993-aa8f-2308ed2df00b" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #