ional power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}={x}^{6/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5+1}}{(\\frac{6}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/5}}{\\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{6/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b264e9f-1b95-5da0-8a5d-53d43f387515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b264e9f-1b95-5da0-8a5d-53d43f387515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503898} :answer-field/id #uuid "7639bdc5-e09a-59de-9a6d-7760ce0dd6a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a04ec978-e44a-5fc0-941a-3437fa7bf03c" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5+1}}{(\\frac{9}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14/5}}{\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\cdot \\frac{14}{5}{x}^{14/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a07fc94f-5b6d-5ea9-93c2-7e82f2229b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07fc94f-5b6d-5ea9-93c2-7e82f2229b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503901} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe4c120-0640-5e64-a81c-759b876005de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a26b65ff-d0b9-55fd-88b5-aeb6bb366617" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}={x}^{8/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5+1}}{(\\frac{8}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13/5}}{\\left(\\frac{13}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{13/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}{x}^{13/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{8/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9b9a59b-1b30-5f5a-96ca-ff44ec806088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b9a59b-1b30-5f5a-96ca-ff44ec806088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503915} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f7164e-c9db-55ed-a2c8-afeccd4f032f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3c79b12-9c14-5549-a389-57292bb076aa" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d457834a-752e-5c58-903b-17caad36936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d457834a-752e-5c58-903b-17caad36936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503912} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2aee605-eea6-5ac3-a7aa-7faf947e835f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9f77bb-3e23-5176-a3ef-3da256c8008e" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181054c9-dbd9-5b5f-a504-255307d12709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "181054c9-dbd9-5b5f-a504-255307d12709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503880} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4ba04d-d537-57d4-bc2d-b413bfc38584" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1ab8645-2279-57cf-bef0-772d2fda5a19" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/3}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3+1}}{(\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/3}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}{x}^{7/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b19525f-2038-5110-92f7-e19997cc0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b19525f-2038-5110-92f7-e19997cc0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503885} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbecae76-7205-578e-830e-6ad24ef696f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e61be54d-6f89-5777-b645-69438909a48d" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2+1}}{(\\frac{7}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}{x}^{9/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138} :answer-field/id #uuid "915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346221" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504032} {:answer/id #uuid "08ee82a2-784d-5e9b-adf7-eadff82d9072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504048} {:answer/id #uuid "24a0b4c2-0c8c-5103-9f4a-eed2189e8fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504095} {:answer/id #uuid "394f7ad5-b9aa-5163-b7f9-a244a184d786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504132} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ef87e-9cad-533d-bf6e-91d974d3d93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504032} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0150772-06cd-545e-9657-56eec3250d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107916" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{cx+4}={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504035} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaccb67-ceda-5dd0-8a10-f61a5a273841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504190} {:answer/id #uuid "b036582e-39f9-5e50-b0db-d7985a363b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504337} {:answer/id #uuid "d3837867-f45b-547f-9d74-b1e884fcfe7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504395} {:answer/id #uuid "de534e5e-3fce-5121-9f25-e5c2f538099b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504035} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b2d790-750c-54c8-8acc-4cf1f5c0b6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" :question/math-academy-id "q-152787" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" :question/math-academy-id "q-340259" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{3}{x+3}\\right) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{3}{x+3}\\right) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359a0b0c-50e8-5345-975b-7a3b5f1bf014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504123} {:answer/id #uuid "45c11c78-2d68-5904-8031-3d3a0242f7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504158} {:answer/id #uuid "ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504382} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0f3d27-f2c4-53e6-a0dd-86a7157d57b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504416} {:answer/id #uuid "ee53d443-4dcf-58ab-8684-f164d88801ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504382} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c67064-cabf-594b-8138-f2a53ecff042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94622" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504100} {:answer/id #uuid "4776454d-1433-55b0-a3f1-6744fa92a4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504161} {:answer/id #uuid "869d1c4e-d590-57e1-8c7b-108e1a80a41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504267} {:answer/id #uuid "a85269ed-a160-5cdf-ba28-b8d04e6cc4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504325} {:answer/id #uuid "e5adc569-5d12-5e17-a6da-903c09507f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504100} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc30e01e-9aef-5e28-9a4c-abb1934ac9fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106854" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07850059-ddb8-59d8-9ab0-8177d5206ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504046} {:answer/id #uuid "4d11d421-27fe-5c53-897b-17c35864bd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504171} {:answer/id #uuid "c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504361} {:answer/id #uuid "c969f3b4-9799-57d4-a8f4-6cf2a9b701dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504372} {:answer/id #uuid "f5168c4d-1a9d-5db6-b1eb-f2e01f7135ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504361} :answer-field/id #uuid "65db8db6-731c-5056-8281-d2ccd26fd053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152891" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right))=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504300} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504351} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504374} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504385} {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-104854" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504057} {:answer/id #uuid "672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504213} {:answer/id #uuid "83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504259} {:answer/id #uuid "896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504270} {:answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302} :answer-field/id #uuid "264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338347" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-341951" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1fc9040-4cdf-577b-b242-fcc38c2ab2bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" :question/math-academy-id "q-302680" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a368caa-071a-5e09-b895-f6fee3f0b562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504217} {:answer/id #uuid "9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504292} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b7b665-8ee1-53eb-8f95-7a0b77e77444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504326} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ce9c8-7667-5765-b9a9-b725886916c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504366} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf1a8b-3c3d-5e6c-971a-9dd68e5b7b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504292} :answer-field/id #uuid "726c20fa-e270-57b1-9453-2ca197a81f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107915" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x-2}={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165} :answer-field/id #uuid "0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340282" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466} :answer-field/id #uuid "70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-334837" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\right)=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504317} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc400da-c09d-518f-b91a-2b22630e2790" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339480" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504243} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fcb170-b0cc-55d0-ae9f-7fe8d73132e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344539" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504031} {:answer/id #uuid "22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504092} {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} {:answer/id #uuid "a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504322} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{\\pi n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-113931" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450} :answer-field/id #uuid "d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346208" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341713" :question/problem "Given that $y=5e{x}^{1/x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504083} {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} {:answer/id #uuid "4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504169} {:answer/id #uuid "59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No infinite discontinuities" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504188} {:answer/id #uuid "f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$ and $x=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-104860" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+8}\\right) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+8}\\right) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504281} {:answer/id #uuid "98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504303} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504314} {:answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} {:answer/id #uuid "f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338353" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9dcace-dd91-581c-866e-f6d1aa1b2cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504770} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb644e7-b21f-5fce-8ff0-497d610ecaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504775} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf206f0-3208-5daf-92b4-982429991a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504798} {:answer/id #uuid "8355501f-d15e-5901-913e-d30d08a04672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504800} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b2c6bf-5dbe-54b3-abdf-e2d66204b501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bb644e7-b21f-5fce-8ff0-497d610ecaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504775} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e7c313-092b-5316-b111-b5874ef1f27d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14ea91d3-f94c-5dd9-b51c-cb8c953c07f2" :question/problem "Given that $-3a - 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-3a - 24b=-6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3f1c2f-be82-53cd-aff4-7e5521c7167f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504749} {:answer/id #uuid "2eca6419-7234-51d4-b873-e7f3bd1bacf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504772} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4d6bea-566b-5d1a-8261-205958a929e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504784} {:answer/id #uuid "6aeefdc9-79a6-53eb-956a-d3afdf335860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504794} {:answer/id #uuid "f889768d-c9e5-5cf4-906b-9103278e26a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4d6bea-566b-5d1a-8261-205958a929e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504784} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de5c44f-68ff-569f-b35c-cf4b217b5b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281e43ed-d77f-5100-ba06-3efb56464379" :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.25a - 3b)$ and $-a + 12b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)a+λ(-3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{4}} = -4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{12}{-3} = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a + 12b=-4(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d92bf8b-f09a-519e-b6c2-297a8dbbae50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504757} {:answer/id #uuid "6742f50f-0fc1-5cef-9bfc-a345a595db6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504793} {:answer/id #uuid "a142d2dd-1c1e-5f0b-b6cf-e6016f0ff340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504808} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bea5f0-8a2f-5d26-b298-26810baae035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504813} {:answer/id #uuid "fd39139a-489f-500a-bb43-0430f464ded4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bea5f0-8a2f-5d26-b298-26810baae035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504813} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac2129e-641b-514b-a9a0-d474e422aab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e813b171-b626-57db-af36-b0b2e170a174" :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.5a + 3b)$ and $-a - 6b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 6b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bd2dae-9f93-5a40-8d4b-eef040995cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504744} {:answer/id #uuid "363e5073-dc2e-5bbf-8201-fe87b22042bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504774} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2d54b2-1153-5fc4-9a0e-c048e849ed9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504777} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd349ad-f11c-561b-b033-1a228c5244e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504789} {:answer/id #uuid "b507226d-14fb-5b7f-9ec4-42ba988fbde3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd349ad-f11c-561b-b033-1a228c5244e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504789} :answer-field/id #uuid "22efb133-43d7-5ecd-b9af-451a0d3ef57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0dc3593-c754-5805-816a-41c4fccabc8f" :question/problem "Given that $3a + 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n3a + 24b=6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e0fae-7ea0-5e55-aa28-b1cf2c56b250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504754} {:answer/id #uuid "63109784-3c77-52c6-a07b-5fa548fbd809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504790} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8036ee-262d-5fb7-9adf-3a8cb2f1b4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504795} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad182c-2996-57a2-982a-7cc91e8a14de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504805} {:answer/id #uuid "bf945500-ba30-51dc-a6bf-5f492b1428ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8036ee-262d-5fb7-9adf-3a8cb2f1b4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504795} :answer-field/id #uuid "291021b9-5572-5413-9bb4-fde113118354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e435c8-cd66-5b3b-b59e-c0b5f48a6d99" :question/problem "Given that $-a - 2b$ and $λ(0.5a + b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-2}{1} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 2b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341314" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" :question/math-academy-id "q-342289" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right)}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}\\right)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{m}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341486" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,\\left(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} {:answer/id #uuid "570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505280} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-2}{y+2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505527} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y+2}{y-2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505533} {:answer/id #uuid "ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2+y}{2-y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} :answer-field/id #uuid "021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" :question/math-academy-id "q-126259" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\in (-2,2),$ which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
